thread_local
(C++11)
A variable declared with the thread_local
specifier is accessible only on the thread on which it's created. The variable is created when the thread is created, and it's destroyed when the thread is destroyed. Each thread has its own copy of the variable. On Windows, thread_local
is functionally equivalent to the Microsoft-specific __declspec( thread )
attribute.
thread_local float f = 42.0; // Global namespace. Not implicitly static.
